ef 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172018 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172082 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1045 "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172172 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dd0bd3a2-aeb8-58a5-aac7-31019071b253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/32165a622d57d6af278b7ce288093ef4db6867619046b8625071f0274a1c7e93.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0a5efdc-b40e-5dde-9c9f-7f214338581c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172601 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/a2/a278676af009b7096bd50f9b3918f0e8090c1822a9e029ac2b4b6325d1ba7a4c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}={x}^{3}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172868 #edb/ref 1061 25710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172868 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c590d4ee-83a9-5629-b80d-5b9bf0fef93a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172868 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172869 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172869 #edb/ref 1061 25712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df729ab0-2123-5f4d-8bcf-9b5d2fb448d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172870 #edb/ref 1061 26196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf643e99-f61d-5e66-8afd-c5e23c2c54df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172979 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172979 #edb/ref 1061 34802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172979 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9018b840-14fc-5403-a85b-0696a121416b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172979 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172982 #edb/ref 1061 35034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de33aa28-2c66-5a35-b972-8287ff822b59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173195 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the differential equation $y' = -4xy.$ The slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a47ec9497553fdcc9d20607f309bdaee00bb1fc0af4d39dea8a6a843a2e417e6.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(x,y).$ Indeed, the equation $y' =-4xy$ is of this form.\n\n- Second, notice that the solution curves appear to have stationary points along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$ This means that the right-hand side of our differential equation must evaluate to zero on these lines. Indeed, this is true of our equation.\n\n![](images/7b/7beccfd6840c2715bfd3c923f4fe6ac05bdf07f9ed65931b016efd937b1a0b63.png)\n\n- Third, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ split the plane into four regions. The slopes of the solution curves in each of these regions are summarized in the table below.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <       0$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n|  | $x<       0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nNotice that the sign of $f(x,y)$ in the four regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is positive in the second and fourth quadrants, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is negative in the first and third quadrants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173195 #edb/ref 1065 5672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173195 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dd16b3e1-53b3-55bf-af84-540daaefde15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173195 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186536905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186536922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186536925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186536970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186536976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1045 "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186536925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536271 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "430421d0-b0f8-5943-9c2b-aea2664e5f42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536285 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0026c51e-4d80-5bc2-8461-49af596e4e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536286 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "007e39a0-05eb-578a-a48d-5694fcd3c657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536314 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{′}=xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536314 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536314 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12ba6730-d2d1-5ea5-80ef-8e8c38878511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536339 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536339 #edb/ref 1082 #e